---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../components/CaseSection.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import Glance from "../../components/Glance.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="itonics-design-system"
  team="6 people incl. PM & developers"
  readTime="3 min read"
  coverPlain
  coverWidth="max-w-[806px]"
  coverSpacing="mt-4 mb-4 sm:mt-7 sm:mb-7"
  externalCategory="External"
  externalLabel="itonics-innovation.com"
  externalHref="https://www.itonics-innovation.com/market-intelligence-demo"
>
  <Glance>
    <Fragment slot="challenge">
      ITONICS Cloud inherited components from a previous desktop version. Old and
      new components mixed together created inconsistencies in style and
      behaviour, and developers wasted time re-coding similar components.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="strategy">
      A unified, standardised, reusable system — built on Atomic Design — with a
      style guide, consolidated components, and a maintained UI library.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="impact">
      One source of truth for designers and developers: a consistent experience,
      clearer communication, faster low-fi iteration, and fewer redundant
      components.
    </Fragment>
  </Glance>

  <CaseSection
    num="01"
    title="ITONICS Cloud inherited a patchwork of desktop components no one could reuse."
  >
    <Prose>
      <p>
        ITONICS Cloud is a SaaS solution for innovation management that helps
        users analyse data and translate it into insights. The Cloud had some
        pre-existing components inherited from the previous desktop version. We
        needed unified, standardised, and reusable rules to follow for the Cloud
        version.
      </p>
      <h3>The challenges</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <span class="block font-semibold text-[#0041d9]"
            >Inconsistent &amp; incoherent</span
          >
          Old desktop-version components mixed with new components resulted in
          inconsistencies and incoherence in styles and behaviours.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="block font-semibold text-[#0041d9]"
            >Rebuilding the same components</span
          >
          Developers were wasting time re-coding similar components that could
          have been consolidated into one.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
    <figure class="mx-auto mt-12 w-[70%]">
      <img
        src="/images/itonics-design-system/uis.png"
        alt=""
        loading="lazy"
        class="w-full rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
      />
      <figcaption
        class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
      >
        The existing interface — mixed, inconsistent components
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    num="02"
    title="So I built one system — consistent, documented, and reusable."
  >
    <Prose>
      <h3>What the design system should achieve</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Achieve a consistent user experience</li>
        <li>
          Provide clarity that improves communication between developers and
          designers
        </li>
        <li>
          Allow faster iteration — more frequent low-fi mockups, applying the
          design system once we reach a concrete design decision
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>How I approached it, in four steps</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Inventory audit, to evaluate the experience and identify issues</li>
        <li>
          Create a style guide; specify UX rules and component usage where none
          existed
        </li>
        <li>Consolidate extraneous or overlapping components and patterns</li>
        <li>Document and maintain the UI library</li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    num="03"
    title="I audited the UI by purpose, not appearance — to find what actually needed standardizing."
  >
    <Prose>
      <p>
        <strong class="block">UX auditing.</strong> The way I group elements is based on their
        purpose and type, rather than appearance. This aligns with how we organise
        the component library. Through this purpose-directed inventory, we can see
        an overview of how elements are used in the system, what the behaviours
        are, and what needs to be standardised.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/itonics-design-system/audit.png"
      caption="Purpose-directed inventory audit"
    />
    <Prose class="mt-20">
      <p>
        <strong class="block">Establish rules and guidelines.</strong> After the audit, I
        capture the existing patterns or determine the best practices to follow
        as rules. I follow the Atomic Design approach (Brad Frost, 2016) to
        organise our design system — with folders like Atoms, Molecules,
        Organisms, Templates, Pages, and Extra Content.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/itonics-design-system/rules.png"
      caption="Rules and guidelines, organised with Atomic Design"
    />
    <Callout>
      Grouping by purpose — not appearance — is what let the audit map directly
      onto a reusable component library.
    </Callout>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    num="04"
    title="The output: a style guide, a component library, and specs the team could build from."
  >
    <div class="grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-[1.1fr_1fr] md:items-start">
      <div>
        <Prose>
          <ul>
            <li>Style guide and pattern library</li>
            <li>Design specifications</li>
            <li>A UI library built in Sketch to house the design files</li>
            <li>System maintenance and updates</li>
          </ul>
        </Prose>
        <Callout>
          A shared library meant designers and engineers stopped rebuilding the
          same components — and started speaking the same language.
        </Callout>
      </div>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/itonics-design-system/detail-pages.png"
          alt=""
          loading="lazy"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        />
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          Design deliverable — entity detail page
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <Prose class="mt-16">
      <h3>Specs &amp; components</h3>
      <p>
        I documented each element with build-ready specs and consolidated the
        one-off variants into reusable components.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/itonics-design-system/specs.png"
      caption="Design specification — element hero banner"
    />
    <Figure
      src="/images/itonics-design-system/cards.png"
      caption="Design deliverable — card component"
    />
    <Prose class="mt-16">
      <h3>Before &amp; after</h3>
      <p>
        The same interface, rebuilt on the system — consistent spacing,
        typography, and behaviour.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <div class="my-8 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/itonics-design-system/before.png"
          alt="Before"
          loading="lazy"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        />
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          Before
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/itonics-design-system/after.png"
          alt="After"
          loading="lazy"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        />
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          After
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    num="05"
    title="What building a design system solo taught me."
  >
    <Prose>
      <p>
        <strong>Establish rituals and processes to move things forward.</strong>
        Since I was the only designer on the team, I could only work on the design
        system in parallel with my day-to-day tasks. So I set up a roadmap and
        checkpoints for its development. This helped me move things forward and
        break the work into smaller, more manageable chunks.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Have the design system in mind.</strong> Always inspect what you
        can build for the design system when you have a design task. On the other
        hand, check what you already have in the system before making any new
        design.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A design system is a living, ongoing project.</strong> Sometimes
        the insufficiency of the guidelines shows during implementation. So
        updating and improving the design system is just as important as creating
        it.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudy>
